1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,936
Two to four kilometers
beneath the North Sea,

2
00:00:03,936 --> 00:00:08,010
oil and gas sit in porous rock at
pressures of several hundred bar.

3
00:00:08,710 --> 00:00:13,310
A well is the only engineered path
between that energy and the world above.

4
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:18,288
For decades of production, and then,
once it is plugged, for an eternity,

5
00:00:18,288 --> 00:00:22,860
that path must stay closed to everything
except what we intend to let through.

6
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:25,530
That is the job of well integrity.

7
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:31,477
In this film we look at how it is done
on the Norwegian Continental Shelf:

8
00:00:31,477 --> 00:00:34,868
the regulations and standards,
the barrier philosophy,

9
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:37,380
how barriers are verified and monitored,

10
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:41,964
how integrity is classified across a
portfolio of wells, what goes wrong,

11
00:00:41,964 --> 00:00:45,041
and what the industry is
learning as thousands of

12
00:00:45,041 --> 00:00:47,930
wells approach permanent
plug and abandonment.

13
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:57,032
NORSOK D-010, the Norwegian
well integrity standard,

14
00:00:57,032 --> 00:01:00,838
defines well integrity as
the application of technical,

15
00:01:00,838 --> 00:01:04,574
operational and organizational
solutions to reduce the

16
00:01:04,574 --> 00:01:09,072
risk of uncontrolled release of
formation fluids and well fluids,

17
00:01:09,072 --> 00:01:11,563
throughout the life cycle of a well.

18
00:01:12,263 --> 00:01:17,595
Technical solutions are the hardware: casing,
cement, packers, valves and wellheads,

19
00:01:17,595 --> 00:01:19,943
designed for the loads they will see.

20
00:01:20,643 --> 00:01:25,843
Operational solutions are how the well
is run: procedures, testing, monitoring,

21
00:01:25,843 --> 00:01:30,713
and operating limits that keep every
component inside its design envelope.

22
00:01:31,413 --> 00:01:36,491
And organizational solutions are the people
and the system around them: competence,

23
00:01:36,491 --> 00:01:39,244
clear responsibilities,
management of change,

24
00:01:39,244 --> 00:01:43,343
and documentation that follows the
well from one owner to the next.

25
00:01:44,543 --> 00:01:46,123
The words life cycle matter.

26
00:01:46,423 --> 00:01:48,933
A well is designed, drilled and completed.

27
00:01:49,233 --> 00:01:54,475
It produces or injects, it is intervened
on and worked over, it may be suspended,

28
00:01:54,475 --> 00:01:57,063
and finally it is permanently abandoned.

29
00:01:57,763 --> 00:02:03,697
The international standard ISO sixteen five
thirty frames this as life-cycle governance,

30
00:02:03,697 --> 00:02:09,293
and every handover between phases must
carry a documented, verified barrier status.

31
00:02:09,993 --> 00:02:11,123
Why does this matter?

32
00:02:11,423 --> 00:02:15,968
Because a loss of integrity can mean
a blowout, a release to sea or air,

33
00:02:15,968 --> 00:02:18,429
an underground flow between formations,

34
00:02:18,429 --> 00:02:23,163
or a slow leak that nobody sees until
pressure appears where it should not.

35
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,897
Norwegian petroleum regulation
is largely functional.

36
00:02:32,197 --> 00:02:34,789
The regulations state what must be achieved,

37
00:02:34,789 --> 00:02:39,797
and the guidelines point to recognized
standards that describe one way to achieve it.

38
00:02:40,497 --> 00:02:44,977
The regulator is Havtil, the
Norwegian Ocean Industry Authority,

39
00:02:44,977 --> 00:02:49,247
which until 2024 was known as
the Petroleum Safety Authority.

40
00:02:49,947 --> 00:02:53,234
The Facilities Regulations
require that well barriers

41
00:02:53,234 --> 00:02:56,583
are designed so that well
integrity is ensured and the

42
00:02:56,583 --> 00:03:00,367
barrier functions are safeguarded
during the well's lifetime.

43
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:03,037
Barrier performance must be verifiable.

44
00:03:03,737 --> 00:03:05,295
When a well is abandoned,

45
00:03:05,295 --> 00:03:08,537
its barriers must be designed
for the longest period

46
00:03:08,537 --> 00:03:11,093
the well is expected to remain abandoned,

47
00:03:11,093 --> 00:03:16,267
and it must be possible to cut the casings
safely when the well is finally plugged.

48
00:03:16,967 --> 00:03:20,197
The Activities Regulations
add the operational side.

49
00:03:20,497 --> 00:03:23,442
During drilling and well
activities there shall be

50
00:03:23,442 --> 00:03:26,387
tested well barriers with
sufficient independence.

51
00:03:26,687 --> 00:03:27,797
If a barrier fails,

52
00:03:27,797 --> 00:03:32,767
no other activities may be carried out in
the well than those intended to restore it.

53
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:37,752
And when a well is handed over,
its barrier status shall be tested,

54
00:03:37,752 --> 00:03:39,287
verified and documented.

55
00:03:39,987 --> 00:03:41,093
And above them all,

56
00:03:41,093 --> 00:03:45,753
the Management Regulations require that
barriers are established and maintained,

57
00:03:45,753 --> 00:03:49,714
and that it is known which barriers
exist, what function they serve,

58
00:03:49,714 --> 00:03:53,617
what performance is required of
them, and whether they are working.

59
00:03:54,317 --> 00:03:57,931
For all of this, the guidelines
point to NORSOK D-010,

60
00:03:57,931 --> 00:04:01,077
Well integrity in drilling
and well operations.

61
00:04:01,377 --> 00:04:04,136
Revision five was published in 2021,

62
00:04:04,136 --> 00:04:09,807
and it is now issued in a consolidated
edition with corrections from 2024.

63
00:04:10,507 --> 00:04:15,673
Revision five reorganized the standard
and added a barrier requirements matrix,

64
00:04:15,673 --> 00:04:21,558
premises for using creeping formation and
materials other than cement as barrier elements,

65
00:04:21,558 --> 00:04:24,958
accepted methods such as
perforate, wash and cement,

66
00:04:24,958 --> 00:04:28,162
an expanded chapter on
managed pressure drilling,

67
00:04:28,162 --> 00:04:31,367
and a formal route for
qualifying new technology.

68
00:04:32,067 --> 00:04:35,612
Around the standard sit the
Offshore Norge guidelines:

69
00:04:35,612 --> 00:04:40,010
guideline one-seventeen on well
integrity, covering classification,

70
00:04:40,010 --> 00:04:42,768
annulus pressure management and handovers,

71
00:04:42,768 --> 00:04:46,247
and guideline one-thirty-five
on how well control and

72
00:04:46,247 --> 00:04:49,727
well integrity incidents
are classified and reported.

73
00:04:50,427 --> 00:04:53,799
Operators share experience
through industry forums for

74
00:04:53,799 --> 00:04:56,547
well integrity and for plug and abandonment,

75
00:04:56,547 --> 00:05:01,357
so that a lesson learned on one field
can protect wells on every other field.

76
00:05:06,667 --> 00:05:09,980
At the heart of NORSOK
D-010 is the well barrier:

77
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:13,428
an envelope of one or
several well barrier elements

78
00:05:13,428 --> 00:05:16,200
that together prevent fluids from flowing

79
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,648
unintentionally from a
formation into the wellbore,

80
00:05:19,648 --> 00:05:23,367
into another formation, or
to the external environment.

81
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:27,297
A well barrier element, on
its own, does not stop flow.

82
00:05:27,597 --> 00:05:32,310
A casing joint with no cement behind
it, or a valve with no body around it,

83
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:33,567
is just a component.

84
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:36,737
Only the complete, closed
envelope is a barrier.

85
00:05:37,437 --> 00:05:40,287
The core principle is
two independent barriers.

86
00:05:40,587 --> 00:05:45,207
The primary well barrier is the first
envelope that prevents flow from a source.

87
00:05:45,507 --> 00:05:49,787
The secondary well barrier is the one
that takes over if the primary fails.

88
00:05:50,487 --> 00:05:52,577
Ideally the two envelopes share nothing.

89
00:05:52,877 --> 00:05:56,781
Where an element is part of both,
a common well barrier element,

90
00:05:56,781 --> 00:05:59,587
the extra risk must be
assessed and mitigated.

91
00:06:00,887 --> 00:06:02,727
Look first at a well being drilled.

92
00:06:03,027 --> 00:06:06,067
Here the primary barrier is
the drilling fluid itself.

93
00:06:06,367 --> 00:06:09,607
The weight of the mud column
holds back the formation pressure.

94
00:06:10,307 --> 00:06:11,497
The physics is simple.

95
00:06:11,797 --> 00:06:16,517
Hydrostatic pressure equals density
times gravity times true vertical depth.

96
00:06:16,817 --> 00:06:21,385
Mud with a specific gravity of one
point three, at three thousand meters,

97
00:06:21,385 --> 00:06:26,705
exerts about three hundred and eighty bar,
comfortably above a pore pressure of, say,

98
00:06:26,705 --> 00:06:28,207
three hundred and fifty.

99
00:06:28,907 --> 00:06:34,105
Behind the fluid stands the secondary barrier:
the formation at the last casing shoe,

100
00:06:34,105 --> 00:06:38,447
the casing cement, the casing, the
wellhead, and the blowout preventer.

101
00:06:39,147 --> 00:06:44,147
The formation at the casing shoe is a barrier
element too, and its strength must be known.

102
00:06:44,447 --> 00:06:45,990
After each casing is set,

103
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:49,200
a few meters of new hole
are drilled and the well is

104
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:53,027
pressured up in a formation
integrity test or a leak-off test.

105
00:06:53,327 --> 00:06:56,208
The pressure at which the
rock begins to take fluid

106
00:06:56,208 --> 00:06:58,468
sets the upper limit for the mud weight,

107
00:06:58,468 --> 00:07:01,407
and for the pressure the
secondary barrier can hold.

108
00:07:02,107 --> 00:07:03,730
If the fluid column fails,

109
00:07:03,730 --> 00:07:08,788
for example through losses to a weak
formation or by swabbing while pulling pipe,

110
00:07:08,788 --> 00:07:10,787
formation fluid enters the well.

111
00:07:11,087 --> 00:07:12,847
Gas expands as it rises.

112
00:07:13,547 --> 00:07:17,097
The crew detects the influx and
closes the blowout preventer.

113
00:07:17,397 --> 00:07:20,175
The secondary barrier
now holds the pressure,

114
00:07:20,175 --> 00:07:23,386
while the primary barrier
is restored by circulating

115
00:07:23,386 --> 00:07:27,277
out the influx and replacing the
mud with a heavier kill fluid.

116
00:07:27,977 --> 00:07:31,306
In managed pressure drilling
the fluid column is lighter,

117
00:07:31,306 --> 00:07:34,227
and a closed system adds
back-pressure at surface.

118
00:07:34,527 --> 00:07:40,487
Revision five of D-010 expanded the
requirements for exactly this kind of operation.

119
00:07:41,787 --> 00:07:44,297
Now the same well, completed and producing.

120
00:07:44,597 --> 00:07:48,077
The fluid column is gone, so the
envelopes are made of hardware.

121
00:07:48,777 --> 00:07:54,196
The primary barrier is drawn in blue: the
casing and cement below the production packer,

122
00:07:54,196 --> 00:07:59,307
the production packer itself, the production
tubing, and the downhole safety valve.

123
00:08:00,007 --> 00:08:02,867
The downhole safety valve
deserves a closer look.

124
00:08:03,167 --> 00:08:08,197
It is a fail-safe flapper valve, held
open by hydraulic pressure from surface.

125
00:08:08,497 --> 00:08:11,158
If that pressure is
lost, a spring closes it,

126
00:08:11,158 --> 00:08:14,707
shutting in the well even if
everything above it is damaged.

127
00:08:15,407 --> 00:08:21,105
The secondary barrier is drawn in red: the cap
rock and casing cement above the reservoir,

128
00:08:21,105 --> 00:08:25,854
the production casing, the wellhead,
the tubing hanger, the annulus valves,

129
00:08:25,854 --> 00:08:28,577
and the master valve on the Christmas tree.

130
00:08:29,277 --> 00:08:31,457
This drawing is a well barrier schematic.

131
00:08:31,757 --> 00:08:37,509
D-010 requires one for every well activity
and phase, showing each element, its status,

132
00:08:37,509 --> 00:08:43,327
and the formation strength, so that anyone
can see at a glance what is holding the well.

133
00:08:44,027 --> 00:08:49,521
Each element type has an element acceptance
table in the standard, stating its function,

134
00:08:49,521 --> 00:08:54,703
how it must be designed and selected, how
it is tested and verified when installed,

135
00:08:54,703 --> 00:08:57,887
and how it must be monitored
and maintained in use.

136
00:08:58,587 --> 00:09:00,977
And the envelopes change
with every activity.

137
00:09:01,277 --> 00:09:04,309
During a wireline or
coiled tubing intervention,

138
00:09:04,309 --> 00:09:07,721
the pressure control equipment
rigged up on top of the

139
00:09:07,721 --> 00:09:10,438
tree becomes part of the barrier envelopes,

140
00:09:10,438 --> 00:09:15,177
and each change of configuration must
be reflected in an updated schematic.

141
00:09:20,467 --> 00:09:23,527
A barrier that has not been
verified is an assumption.

142
00:09:23,827 --> 00:09:27,817
Verification turns it into a fact,
recorded for the life of the well.

143
00:09:28,517 --> 00:09:32,847
The most common verification is a
pressure test in the direction of flow.

144
00:09:33,147 --> 00:09:38,828
D-010 calls for a low pressure test of fifteen
to twenty bar for at least five minutes,

145
00:09:38,828 --> 00:09:40,787
before the high pressure test.

146
00:09:41,487 --> 00:09:46,012
The static test pressure is then observed
and recorded for at least ten minutes,

147
00:09:46,012 --> 00:09:47,257
with a stable reading.

148
00:09:47,557 --> 00:09:51,092
Unless the acceptance table for
that element says otherwise,

149
00:09:51,092 --> 00:09:53,037
the acceptable leak rate is zero.

150
00:09:53,737 --> 00:09:58,607
A falling curve that never flattens tells
a different story: something is leaking,

151
00:09:58,607 --> 00:10:02,527
and the barrier is not accepted
until the leak is found and fixed.

152
00:10:03,227 --> 00:10:05,877
Some elements are better
tested from the other side.

153
00:10:06,177 --> 00:10:09,810
In an inflow test, the pressure
above the barrier is reduced,

154
00:10:09,810 --> 00:10:11,657
simulating the load from below.

155
00:10:11,957 --> 00:10:15,997
If nothing flows and the pressure does
not build up, the barrier is holding.

156
00:10:16,297 --> 00:10:19,687
D-010 calls for at least thirty
minutes of stable reading.

157
00:10:20,387 --> 00:10:22,687
Interpretation is part of the barrier.

158
00:10:22,987 --> 00:10:26,650
In the Macondo disaster in
the Gulf of Mexico in 2010,

159
00:10:26,650 --> 00:10:32,417
a negative pressure test was misread as a
success, and the well blew out hours later.

160
00:10:33,117 --> 00:10:37,218
Valves with an allowable leak rate,
such as the downhole safety valve,

161
00:10:37,218 --> 00:10:40,617
are tested by closing the valve
and bleeding off above it.

162
00:10:40,917 --> 00:10:44,867
The pressure build-up in the closed
volume is converted to a leak rate.

163
00:10:45,167 --> 00:10:50,827
In the production phase, D-010 recommends a
differential of seventy bar for these tests.

164
00:10:51,527 --> 00:10:57,097
The blowout preventer is tested too: function
tested and pressure tested when installed,

165
00:10:57,097 --> 00:10:58,997
and then at regular intervals.

166
00:10:59,297 --> 00:11:04,247
Revision five of D-010 kept the
fourteen-day interval for the pressure test.

167
00:11:04,947 --> 00:11:06,097
Cement is harder.

168
00:11:06,397 --> 00:11:10,510
We cannot see it, so we infer it:
from the job itself, from volumes,

169
00:11:10,510 --> 00:11:14,019
pressures and returns, from a
pressure test of the casing,

170
00:11:14,019 --> 00:11:17,347
from the formation strength
at the shoe, and from logs.

171
00:11:18,047 --> 00:11:21,497
A cement bond log sends a
sonic pulse along the casing.

172
00:11:21,797 --> 00:11:24,907
Free pipe rings like a bell,
so the amplitude is high.

173
00:11:25,207 --> 00:11:29,647
Casing that is well bonded to cement
is damped, so the amplitude is low.

174
00:11:30,347 --> 00:11:32,077
Ultrasonic tools go further.

175
00:11:32,377 --> 00:11:38,127
They spin a transducer to map the acoustic
impedance behind the casing all the way around,

176
00:11:38,127 --> 00:11:42,727
and they can reveal a channel of mud
that a bond log would average away.

177
00:11:43,427 --> 00:11:47,205
D-010 sets minimum lengths
for cement in the annulus,

178
00:11:47,205 --> 00:11:53,407
longer when it is verified only by volumes,
and shorter when bond is confirmed by logs.

179
00:11:53,707 --> 00:11:55,302
For permanent abandonment,

180
00:11:55,302 --> 00:11:58,431
the external cement must
be verified over a defined

181
00:11:58,431 --> 00:12:01,867
interval before it can be
credited as a barrier element.

182
00:12:02,567 --> 00:12:07,021
Every test is recorded, signed off and
linked to the well barrier schematic,

183
00:12:07,021 --> 00:12:11,301
so that whoever takes over the well
knows not only what the barriers are,

184
00:12:11,301 --> 00:12:13,587
but how and when they were last proven.

185
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:21,960
A producing well is a set
of nested pressure vessels.

186
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:26,000
Between the tubing and the
production casing is the A-annulus.

187
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:30,553
Between the production casing and
the next string out is the B-annulus,

188
00:12:30,553 --> 00:12:32,350
and beyond that the C-annulus.

189
00:12:33,050 --> 00:12:35,950
Pressure in an annulus can
come from three places.

190
00:12:36,650 --> 00:12:39,985
Heat: when hot production
fluid flows up the tubing,

191
00:12:39,985 --> 00:12:42,936
the trapped fluid in a
sealed annulus expands,

192
00:12:42,936 --> 00:12:46,400
and pressure can rise by
tens or even hundreds of bar.

193
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:52,350
In subsea wells, the outer annuli are
often sealed and cannot be bled off at all.

194
00:12:52,650 --> 00:12:57,861
Trapped annular pressure must then be
handled in the design, with stronger casing,

195
00:12:57,861 --> 00:13:02,500
burst disks, compressible foam or
nitrogen cushions, or insulated tubing.

196
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:09,130
Applied pressure: for example lift gas injected
into the A-annulus of a gas lifted well.

197
00:13:09,830 --> 00:13:14,843
And leaks: a connection or packer leak
that lets the tubing talk to the annulus,

198
00:13:14,843 --> 00:13:18,290
a casing leak, or gas migrating
up through poor cement.

199
00:13:18,590 --> 00:13:23,469
Pressure that keeps coming back after it is
bled off is called sustained casing pressure,

200
00:13:23,469 --> 00:13:24,840
and it is a warning sign.

201
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:30,770
Every annulus has a limit, the maximum
allowable annulus surface pressure, or MAASP.

202
00:13:31,070 --> 00:13:35,690
It is the highest pressure the annulus
may contain, measured at the wellhead,

203
00:13:35,690 --> 00:13:39,410
without compromising any well
barrier element in that annulus.

204
00:13:40,110 --> 00:13:44,627
MAASP is found from the weakest point:
the burst rating of the outer casing,

205
00:13:44,627 --> 00:13:49,679
the collapse rating of the inner string,
the rating of the packer, the tubing hanger,

206
00:13:49,679 --> 00:13:54,018
the wellhead and the valves, and,
where the annulus is open to formation,

207
00:13:54,018 --> 00:13:56,930
the strength of the
formation at the casing shoe.

208
00:13:57,630 --> 00:13:59,729
Each limit is evaluated at depth,

209
00:13:59,729 --> 00:14:03,420
corrected for the fluids inside
the annulus and behind it,

210
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:06,410
and derated for temperature
and design factors.

211
00:14:06,710 --> 00:14:10,420
Raise the surface pressure and the
pressure profile moves to the right.

212
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:15,265
The surface pressure at which it first
touches a limit, anywhere along the well,

213
00:14:15,265 --> 00:14:16,970
is the MAASP for that annulus.

214
00:14:17,670 --> 00:14:21,581
Below MAASP the operator sets
operating limits and alarm levels,

215
00:14:21,581 --> 00:14:25,370
so that pressure is managed long
before a limit is approached.

216
00:14:26,070 --> 00:14:27,770
Monitoring is continuous.

217
00:14:28,070 --> 00:14:30,610
Here is the A-annulus of a well starting up.

218
00:14:30,910 --> 00:14:34,850
The pressure rises with temperature,
is bled down, and stabilizes.

219
00:14:35,150 --> 00:14:36,600
That is thermal behavior.

220
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:39,170
Months later the pattern changes.

221
00:14:39,470 --> 00:14:43,429
After every bleed-off the pressure
climbs back toward tubing pressure,

222
00:14:43,429 --> 00:14:44,560
and gas is returned.

223
00:14:44,860 --> 00:14:49,820
That is communication between tubing and
annulus: the primary barrier has failed.

224
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:52,890
The well is now relying on a single barrier.

225
00:14:53,190 --> 00:14:58,140
It is reclassified, risk-reducing measures
are put in place, and the repair is planned.

226
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:00,580
The cost of not watching is real.

227
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:02,339
On Gullfaks C in 2010,

228
00:15:02,339 --> 00:15:07,247
pressure in the C-annulus had been
rising for weeks without being noticed,

229
00:15:07,247 --> 00:15:11,690
before a hole in the casing led to
a serious well control incident.

230
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,547
With hundreds of wells on a
field and thousands on the shelf,

231
00:15:20,547 --> 00:15:22,990
operators need a shared language for risk.

232
00:15:23,290 --> 00:15:27,800
Offshore Norge guideline one-seventeen
provides one, using four colors.

233
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:29,940
Green: a healthy well.

234
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:34,380
The barrier philosophy is intact,
with no or only minor issues.

235
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:38,690
Yellow: one barrier is degraded,
but the other is intact.

236
00:15:38,990 --> 00:15:41,940
The risk is increased, but
within the regulations.

237
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:46,104
Orange: one barrier has failed
while the other is intact,

238
00:15:46,104 --> 00:15:49,750
or a single further failure
could lead to a leak to surface.

239
00:15:50,450 --> 00:15:54,825
Red: one barrier has failed and the
other is degraded or not verified,

240
00:15:54,825 --> 00:15:56,700
or there is a leak to surface.

241
00:15:57,400 --> 00:16:02,074
The category drives priorities: which wells
are repaired first, which are shut in,

242
00:16:02,074 --> 00:16:05,380
and what compensating measures
are needed in the meantime.

243
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:09,727
Havtil also follows the
industry over time through its

244
00:16:09,727 --> 00:16:14,049
annual survey of trends in risk
level in the petroleum activity,

245
00:16:14,049 --> 00:16:20,060
known as RNNP, where well control incidents
and well integrity are tracked as indicators.

246
00:16:21,260 --> 00:16:22,670
How healthy is the shelf?

247
00:16:22,970 --> 00:16:28,167
In a pilot study for the Petroleum Safety
Authority, published by Vignes and Aadnøy,

248
00:16:28,167 --> 00:16:33,550
four hundred and six production and injection
wells on twelve facilities were reviewed.

249
00:16:33,850 --> 00:16:38,243
Eighteen percent had a well integrity
failure, issue or uncertainty,

250
00:16:38,243 --> 00:16:42,120
and production tubing was the
most common failing component.

251
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:46,920
Temporarily abandoned and inactive
wells are a particular concern.

252
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:52,820
In Havtil's 2024 survey, four hundred and
eighty-eight such wells were reported,

253
00:16:52,820 --> 00:16:54,990
roughly double the 2022 survey,

254
00:16:54,990 --> 00:16:58,420
partly because inactive
production wells were now

255
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:01,290
included under the EU methane regulation.

256
00:17:01,990 --> 00:17:07,136
Even while temporarily abandoned, a well
must have qualified, independent barriers,

257
00:17:07,136 --> 00:17:09,120
designed for the planned period.

258
00:17:09,420 --> 00:17:13,770
For subsea wells left for more than twelve
months, integrity must be monitored.

259
00:17:14,470 --> 00:17:18,943
Three were classified red, thirty orange,
one hundred and ninety-two yellow,

260
00:17:18,943 --> 00:17:21,180
and two hundred and sixty-three green.

261
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:25,690
The number of red and orange wells
rose from seventeen to thirty-three.

262
00:17:26,390 --> 00:17:30,853
Havtil's expectation is clear: red
wells are to be permanently plugged,

263
00:17:30,853 --> 00:17:35,190
and orange wells either brought back
into use or permanently plugged.

264
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:43,190
Barrier elements fail in recognizable ways.

265
00:17:43,890 --> 00:17:46,390
Tubing corrodes or leaks at its connections.

266
00:17:47,090 --> 00:17:50,080
Safety valves fail to
close, or close but leak.

267
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:56,160
Casing wears during drilling, corrodes in
service, or is deformed by the rock around it.

268
00:17:56,860 --> 00:17:58,832
Cement is placed with channels,

269
00:17:58,832 --> 00:18:03,223
or cracks and debonds under repeated
pressure and temperature cycles,

270
00:18:03,223 --> 00:18:04,750
opening a micro-annulus.

271
00:18:05,450 --> 00:18:07,420
Seals in the wellhead and hanger age.

272
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:09,950
And the reservoir itself can move.

273
00:18:10,250 --> 00:18:13,083
In the chalk fields of
the southern North Sea,

274
00:18:13,083 --> 00:18:17,208
compaction has lowered the seabed
at Ekofisk by around nine meters,

275
00:18:17,208 --> 00:18:20,780
and casing deformation has
been a long-standing challenge.

276
00:18:21,980 --> 00:18:24,580
Three Norwegian incidents
show what is at stake.

277
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:26,929
On Snorre A, in November 2004,

278
00:18:26,929 --> 00:18:32,530
tubing and casing were being pulled from
an injection well to prepare a sidetrack.

279
00:18:32,830 --> 00:18:35,947
The pulling drew gas from
the reservoir into the well,

280
00:18:35,947 --> 00:18:39,815
and it escaped through a section
of worn casing into the formation,

281
00:18:39,815 --> 00:18:42,240
and up to the seabed beneath the platform.

282
00:18:42,940 --> 00:18:47,397
Production was shut down and most of
the crew were evacuated by helicopter,

283
00:18:47,397 --> 00:18:51,260
while a small team brought the
well under control with heavy mud.

284
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:52,900
The gas could have ignited.

285
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:56,972
Investigations pointed to inadequate
planning and risk assessment,

286
00:18:56,972 --> 00:18:59,830
and to governing documents
that were not followed.

287
00:19:00,530 --> 00:19:02,013
On Gullfaks C, in 2010,

288
00:19:02,013 --> 00:19:05,365
a hole in the thirteen and
three-eighths inch casing

289
00:19:05,365 --> 00:19:09,490
during managed pressure drilling
caused losses to the formation,

290
00:19:09,490 --> 00:19:11,940
and then an influx from the reservoir.

291
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:16,387
The investigations found insufficient
technical integrity in the casing,

292
00:19:16,387 --> 00:19:19,670
and it took more than two
months to restore the barriers.

293
00:19:20,370 --> 00:19:23,220
And in September 2025, on the Troll field,

294
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:27,700
a well was being permanently plugged
from the rig Deepsea Bollsta.

295
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:31,297
When the thirteen and
three-eighths inch casing was cut,

296
00:19:31,297 --> 00:19:35,890
gas from the reservoir that had accumulated
behind it was released to the rig.

297
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:39,749
Around nine hundred and thirty
kilograms of gas were released,

298
00:19:39,749 --> 00:19:42,390
with one hundred and
fourteen people on board.

299
00:19:43,090 --> 00:19:45,975
Havtil found a failure in
the well's outer barrier,

300
00:19:45,975 --> 00:19:49,370
and a lack of barriers towards
the reservoir during the cut.

301
00:19:49,670 --> 00:19:54,411
Technology and methods had been used
that were not qualified for the purpose,

302
00:19:54,411 --> 00:19:59,890
and faulty calibration led to log data being
misinterpreted, so the gas was not detected.

303
00:20:00,190 --> 00:20:05,500
Havtil described the incident as one with
the potential to become a major accident.

304
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:10,747
Twenty years apart, the common threads
are the same: understand your barriers,

305
00:20:10,747 --> 00:20:14,420
verify them, plan for what could
go wrong, and follow the plan.

306
00:20:19,733 --> 00:20:23,343
Every well that is drilled must
one day be permanently plugged.

307
00:20:23,643 --> 00:20:25,050
On the Norwegian shelf,

308
00:20:25,050 --> 00:20:28,292
Havtil estimates that around
two thousand two hundred

309
00:20:28,292 --> 00:20:32,757
and fifty wells will need permanent
plug and abandonment sooner or later,

310
00:20:32,757 --> 00:20:35,815
and operators plan to plug
around three hundred of

311
00:20:35,815 --> 00:20:38,323
them within the next five to seven years.

312
00:20:39,023 --> 00:20:40,893
The work is becoming industrial.

313
00:20:41,193 --> 00:20:42,710
Equinor, for example,

314
00:20:42,710 --> 00:20:48,127
has contracted a semi-submersible rig
specially designed for well plugging,

315
00:20:48,127 --> 00:20:52,533
to permanently plug fifteen to
twenty wells a year from 2026.

316
00:20:53,233 --> 00:20:57,610
A permanent plug and abandonment
is often described in three phases:

317
00:20:57,610 --> 00:21:02,823
reservoir abandonment, where the deep
barriers are set; intermediate abandonment,

318
00:21:02,823 --> 00:21:07,329
where shallower sources are isolated
and casing may be cut and pulled;

319
00:21:07,329 --> 00:21:11,963
and finally removal of the wellhead
and conductor, cut below the seabed.

320
00:21:12,663 --> 00:21:14,293
The requirement is demanding.

321
00:21:14,593 --> 00:21:18,090
D-010 states that a permanently
abandoned well shall

322
00:21:18,090 --> 00:21:20,713
be plugged with an eternal perspective.

323
00:21:21,013 --> 00:21:25,613
There will be no one monitoring it, so
the barriers must last indefinitely,

324
00:21:25,613 --> 00:21:30,213
against the pressure the reservoir may
build up again over geological time.

325
00:21:30,913 --> 00:21:35,347
A permanent barrier must extend across
the full cross section of the well,

326
00:21:35,347 --> 00:21:37,923
including every annulus, from rock to rock.

327
00:21:38,223 --> 00:21:40,885
And its base must sit where
the formation is strong

328
00:21:40,885 --> 00:21:43,443
enough to withstand the
potential pressure below.

329
00:21:43,743 --> 00:21:47,723
Place a plug too shallow, and the reservoir
could fracture the rock beneath it.

330
00:21:48,423 --> 00:21:52,635
For each source of inflow there is
a primary and a secondary barrier,

331
00:21:52,635 --> 00:21:57,823
and near the surface a final barrier isolates
the shallow formations from the seabed.

332
00:21:58,523 --> 00:22:03,851
For cement plugs, D-010 revision five
requires one hundred meters in open hole,

333
00:22:03,851 --> 00:22:07,493
with at least fifty meters
above any source of inflow.

334
00:22:07,793 --> 00:22:08,700
In cased hole,

335
00:22:08,700 --> 00:22:13,945
fifty meters is accepted when the plug
sits on a mechanical or cement foundation,

336
00:22:13,945 --> 00:22:15,823
otherwise one hundred meters.

337
00:22:16,523 --> 00:22:22,382
The material must be impermeable, non-shrinking,
able to withstand mechanical loads,

338
00:22:22,382 --> 00:22:28,032
resistant to chemicals such as hydrogen
sulfide, carbon dioxide and hydrocarbons,

339
00:22:28,032 --> 00:22:30,473
and able to bond to steel and rock.

340
00:22:31,173 --> 00:22:35,724
And every plug is verified: tagged, to
confirm that it is where it should be,

341
00:22:35,724 --> 00:22:37,793
and in cased hole, pressure tested.

342
00:22:38,093 --> 00:22:42,393
The barrier behind the casing must
be verified too, usually by logging.

343
00:22:43,593 --> 00:22:45,813
The hard part is usually the annulus.

344
00:22:46,113 --> 00:22:51,333
A cement plug inside the casing does nothing
if the cement outside the casing is missing.

345
00:22:51,633 --> 00:22:55,323
When logs show poor annular cement,
there are three main options.

346
00:22:56,023 --> 00:23:00,116
Section milling grinds away a
length of casing, exposing the rock,

347
00:23:00,116 --> 00:23:03,713
so that a new plug can be set
from formation to formation.

348
00:23:04,013 --> 00:23:08,123
It works, but it is slow and generates
large volumes of steel swarf.

349
00:23:08,823 --> 00:23:12,129
Perforate, wash and cement
shoots holes in the casing,

350
00:23:12,129 --> 00:23:14,333
jets the annulus clean through them,

351
00:23:14,333 --> 00:23:17,883
and then squeezes cement behind
the pipe in a single trip.

352
00:23:18,183 --> 00:23:21,537
Revision five of D-010
recognizes the method,

353
00:23:21,537 --> 00:23:25,933
with defined premises and a
best practice for verification.

354
00:23:26,633 --> 00:23:28,569
Or the casing is cut and pulled,

355
00:23:28,569 --> 00:23:31,655
which is exactly where the
Troll incident shows the

356
00:23:31,655 --> 00:23:34,681
need to keep well control
barriers in place and to

357
00:23:34,681 --> 00:23:37,343
know what is behind the pipe before you cut.

358
00:23:38,543 --> 00:23:40,533
Sometimes the formation does the work.

359
00:23:40,833 --> 00:23:44,109
Certain shales, rich in clay
minerals such as smectite,

360
00:23:44,109 --> 00:23:49,113
are ductile and creep under stress until
they close the annulus and grip the casing.

361
00:23:49,413 --> 00:23:55,143
Creeping shale has been used as a barrier
element on the Norwegian shelf since 2006.

362
00:23:55,843 --> 00:24:00,733
It is verified by logging, and D-010
sets out its acceptance criteria.

363
00:24:01,033 --> 00:24:05,104
In 2025 Havtil published a
knowledge study on creeping shale,

364
00:24:05,104 --> 00:24:08,107
describing it as
potentially an efficient and

365
00:24:08,107 --> 00:24:11,844
cost-effective way to establish
a barrier behind casing,

366
00:24:11,844 --> 00:24:16,983
and pointing to the need for further
research and more specific requirements.

367
00:24:17,683 --> 00:24:20,793
New materials and methods
are being qualified as well.

368
00:24:21,093 --> 00:24:26,250
Bismuth alloys are melted in place
and expand slightly as they solidify;

369
00:24:26,250 --> 00:24:29,903
Aker BP has set dozens of
bismuth plugs on Valhall.

370
00:24:30,203 --> 00:24:32,593
Thermite-based tools that melt casing,

371
00:24:32,593 --> 00:24:37,373
cement and rock into a single seal have
been tested at full scale in Norway.

372
00:24:37,673 --> 00:24:41,349
And logging through tubing, and
through two casing strings at once,

373
00:24:41,349 --> 00:24:43,763
may let more wells be plugged without a rig.

374
00:24:44,463 --> 00:24:47,453
For every new technology,
the principle is the same.

375
00:24:47,753 --> 00:24:50,888
It must be qualified for
its purpose before it can be

376
00:24:50,888 --> 00:24:53,253
credited as a permanent barrier element.

377
00:24:58,567 --> 00:25:01,346
Well integrity is now
protecting something new.

378
00:25:01,646 --> 00:25:02,665
In August 2025,

379
00:25:02,665 --> 00:25:07,826
Northern Lights began injecting carbon
dioxide into the Aurora storage site,

380
00:25:07,826 --> 00:25:12,987
around two and a half kilometers below
the seabed, south of the Troll field.

381
00:25:13,287 --> 00:25:17,123
Norway has stored carbon
dioxide offshore since 1996,

382
00:25:17,123 --> 00:25:19,946
first at Sleipner and later at Snøhvit.

383
00:25:20,646 --> 00:25:23,017
Carbon dioxide changes the load case.

384
00:25:23,317 --> 00:25:25,856
Mixed with water it forms carbonic acid,

385
00:25:25,856 --> 00:25:29,856
which attacks carbon steel and
can degrade conventional cement.

386
00:25:30,557 --> 00:25:36,404
Impurities in captured carbon dioxide,
such as water, oxygen and sulfur compounds,

387
00:25:36,404 --> 00:25:38,757
can make it even more aggressive,

388
00:25:38,757 --> 00:25:42,180
so corrosion resistant
alloys and carbon dioxide

389
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:45,817
resistant cement systems
are used where it matters.

390
00:25:46,517 --> 00:25:49,121
Injection from ships can be intermittent,

391
00:25:49,121 --> 00:25:54,267
so the well sees repeated cooling and
pressure cycles that can open micro-annuli.

392
00:25:54,966 --> 00:25:59,779
And every old well that penetrates the
storage complex, including plugged ones,

393
00:25:59,779 --> 00:26:02,946
becomes a potential leak
path that must be assessed.

394
00:26:03,646 --> 00:26:07,279
A 2024 study by SINTEF for
Standards Norway concluded

395
00:26:07,279 --> 00:26:10,774
that CO₂ wells are built
much like petroleum wells,

396
00:26:10,774 --> 00:26:12,077
but that the fluid,

397
00:26:12,077 --> 00:26:16,943
the load pattern and the longer
lifetime give a different risk picture,

398
00:26:16,943 --> 00:26:20,986
and that this should be addressed
in the revision of D-010.

399
00:26:26,300 --> 00:26:28,870
Well integrity is also becoming digital.

400
00:26:29,170 --> 00:26:30,344
Annulus pressures,

401
00:26:30,344 --> 00:26:35,431
valve tests and barrier status are
streamed into integrity management systems,

402
00:26:35,431 --> 00:26:40,910
where automated analysis flags abnormal
build-up long before a person would spot it.

403
00:26:41,610 --> 00:26:46,978
Digital barrier schematics keep the status
of every element current, and data from logs,

404
00:26:46,978 --> 00:26:50,090
tests and incidents are
shared across the industry.

405
00:26:50,790 --> 00:26:54,761
Behind the software sits a well
integrity management system:

406
00:26:54,761 --> 00:26:59,725
a register of every well and barrier
element, test intervals based on risk,

407
00:26:59,725 --> 00:27:05,020
procedures for handling deviations,
competence requirements, and regular audits.

408
00:27:05,720 --> 00:27:10,220
But the tools are only as good as their
calibration and the people interpreting them.

409
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:15,540
The Troll incident is a reminder that a
log can look convincing and still be wrong.

410
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:17,840
So, to sum up.

411
00:27:18,540 --> 00:27:20,700
Two independent, tested barriers.

412
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:22,970
Always know your envelopes, and draw them.

413
00:27:23,670 --> 00:27:26,380
A barrier that is not
verified is an assumption.

414
00:27:26,680 --> 00:27:28,410
Test, log and document.

415
00:27:29,110 --> 00:27:30,230
Respect the limits.

416
00:27:30,530 --> 00:27:33,370
Know the MAASP of every
annulus and watch the trends.

417
00:27:34,070 --> 00:27:39,580
Classify honestly, and act on degradation
early, before a yellow well becomes red.

418
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:45,200
Plug for eternity: rock to rock, with
qualified materials, verified in place.

419
00:27:45,900 --> 00:27:51,246
And remember that most failures begin in
planning, risk assessment and communication,

420
00:27:51,246 --> 00:27:54,140
long before the steel
or the cement gives way.

421
00:27:55,440 --> 00:27:59,760
A well is a promise to the future: that
what we opened, we can keep closed.
